# ---- Local operator overrides / secrets (NEVER commit) ----
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---- Air-gap bundle + mirrored artifacts (large, regenerated) ----
/bundle/
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tar.zst

# ---- Kubeconfigs / credentials pulled at runtime ----
kubeconfig
*.kubeconfig
/secrets/
*.pem
*.key
!**/testdata/**/*.key

# ---- Java build output ----
target/
*.class
*.jar
!**/.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar

# ---- PlantUML JVM font-cache litter (from an unset HOME in the render container) ----
docs/diagrams/[?]/
docs/diagrams/.check/

# ---- Tooling / editor ----
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
.DS_Store
mise.local.toml

# Jump-box harness runtime (internal kubeconfig)
.jumpbox/

# Operator-local download links (external, licensed artifacts) — not committed
links.md

# Operator-local Broadcom download token (live credential) — NEVER commit
token.md

# Claude Code harness (agent worktrees, local state) — never tracked/scanned
# Ignore the harness's scratch (worktrees, local settings) — but NOT the repo's own agent
# DEFINITIONS: the VKS adversarial reviewer is a STOPPING RULE for every session (see CLAUDE.md),
# so it must travel with the repo. (A negation cannot re-include a file under an EXCLUDED DIR, so
# exclude the CONTENTS, not the directory.)
.claude/*
!.claude/agents/
# The subagent read-only gate is a SAFETY CONTROL, not a preference: without it an adversary can
# (and did) destroy uncommitted work and open a PR unbidden. It must travel with the repo, or it
# protects exactly one machine. `settings.json` is the wiring; `hooks/` is the gate itself.
# (settings.local.json stays ignored — that one IS per-operator.)
!.claude/settings.json
!.claude/hooks/

# Serializes registry-mutating work (see with_registry_lock in scripts/lib/os.sh)
.registry.lock

# Rendered ArgoCD Applications for a TENANT to hand to their platform team (ARGOCD_MECHANISM=request).
/out/
# the sneakernet bundle + its checksum sidecar (multi-GB; never committed)
vks-airgap-cicd-bundle-*.tar
vks-airgap-cicd-bundle-*.tar.*

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# make deps drops this when either half fails, so check-tools can say "deps failed earlier"
.deps-failed

# ---- per-app build artifacts (six apps, six toolchains) --------------------------------------
# Each app dir IS its own Gitea repo, so anything a build produces there would travel to the
# cluster as source. node_modules alone is 601 files: it was committed once, on 2026-08-22, and
# removed in the same session.
#
# NOT ignored, deliberately: package-lock.json / Cargo.lock. Those are the LOCKFILES the offline
# builders install from (`npm ci`, `cargo fetch --locked`), so they must be in the repo or the
# air-gapped build is not reproducible.
# A Go binary built WITHOUT -o lands in the app dir named after the module, has NO extension, and
# is therefore invisible to every pattern above. Measured 2026-08-22: a 13.8 MB unstripped ELF sat
# in apps/go/gowebapp/ , untracked AND unignored, so `git add -A` would have committed it into that
# app's Gitea repo. Ignore the shape (an app dir's own name) rather than one app's name.
apps/go/*/[a-z]*webapp
apps/rust/*/[a-z]*webapp
# `apps/*/*/x` is FIXED DEPTH -- exactly two levels under apps/. It therefore missed
# apps/dotnet/dotnetwebapp/tests/obj/, and a commit swept 178 build artefacts (7,004 lines) into
# the tree. Any app with a nested project -- a test project, a workspace member, a submodule --
# hits the same hole. `apps/**/x` matches at ANY depth.
apps/**/node_modules/
apps/**/target/
apps/**/obj/
apps/**/bin/
apps/**/__pycache__/
apps/**/.pytest_cache/
apps/**/*.egg-info/
apps/**/TestResults/

